# PDF Analysis and Field Inventory

Before beginning substantial implementation, examine `temp/adequacy.pdf` and identify every field, label, section, instruction, selectable option, and repeated data structure contained in the document.

Do not limit the implementation to the fields explicitly named in this specification. The PDF contains additional fields that must be represented in the web form.

For each field identified in the PDF:

- Determine its intended purpose.
- Determine whether it is static for the overall form or belongs to a repeatable specimen-site entry.
- Select an appropriate web input control.
- Preserve logical relationships and groupings between fields.
- Use clear and consistent frontend, API, and database field names.
- Follow existing PIRO interface patterns whenever an equivalent control or component already exists.

Potential controls include:

- Single-line text inputs
- Multiline text areas
- Numeric inputs
- Date or date-time controls
- Checkboxes
- Radio buttons
- Drop-down menus
- Searchable selection controls
- Read-only values
- Repeatable field groups
- Other controls appropriate for the apparent data type and workflow

Before implementation, produce a concise field inventory containing:

1. Displayed field label
2. Proposed internal field name
3. Whether the field is form-level or part of a repeatable site entry
4. Proposed user-interface control
5. Whether the field appears required or optional, when determinable
6. Source of any permitted values
7. Any ambiguity requiring clarification

When the purpose, expected data type, allowable values, required status, or workflow behavior of a field cannot be determined reliably, ask for clarification rather than making an unsupported assumption.

